WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.935 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our next guest is the
award-winning and best-selling

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:03.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
author of the book
"Say Nothing,"

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:05.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
as well as a staff writer
at "The New Yorker."

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:06.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
His new book,
"Empire of Pain:

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
The Secret History
of the Sackler Dynasty,"

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
is available now.
Please welcome back to the sho

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
Patrick Radden Keefe.
Hello, Patrick. How are you?

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hey, great to see you.
-I don't know if you know,

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
but last night on the show
John Oliver was on

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:22.822
and he very much wanted to use
his time here to plug your book.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.557 align:start size:94% position:6%
So that is
the level of excitement

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:27.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
amongst those of us
who are fans of your work.

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:28.895 line:92%
-I thought that was so amazing

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:30.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
I heard about it this morning.
I was super psyched,

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.899 align:start size:94% position:6%
particularly because
he actually did a segment.

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.934 align:start size:94% position:6%
I write in the book
about a segment he did

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
on his show
about the Sackler family.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:39.139
-Well, that's probably why
he was excited about it,

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:40.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
is he just wanted to see
his name in print.

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:43.109 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's fairly typical of him,
I'd say.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.912
-Yeah, I mean, you know the guy.
That all he's about.

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:49.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, one of the things,
the last time you were here,

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:51.518 align:start size:94% position:6%
talking about
your book "Say Nothing,"

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:54.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
I remember us speaking
after it was over

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you saying this is
what you're working on next.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:59.526 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, obviously,
a great deal of research

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:00.527 line:92%
has to go into a book like this.

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:06.132
And a pandemic, it turns out
will hamstring some effort

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:07.467 line:92%
so far as research is concerned.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:11.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
But people being at home
turned out to be advantageous.

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:13.206 line:92%
-Yeah, it was a funny thing.

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I had been gathering
lots of documents,

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:20.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
going to archives, and suddenl
found that I was shut down

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:25.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
I actually had a pretty
interesting 2020 lined up,

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:27.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I was doing
"Say Nothing" stuff.

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.290
And podcasting, I was working on
"Wind of Change."

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:34.828
And then basically all of my
plans got canceled for a yea

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:36.362 line:92%
and I ended up stuck at home

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:40.166
But I did find that the --
people pick up the phone

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you call them
during a pandemic,

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:45.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
which, for a reporter,
is actually pretty helpful

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.607
-And do you think people,
because they were maybe

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:51.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
so thirsty for human contact
were they more talkative

00:01:51.044 --> 00:01:54.247
than they would have been
during a different time?

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:55.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Way more talkative.
The worst thing

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:58.318 size:84% position:42%
about being a reporter
is cold-calling people,

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:01.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
particularly when all you have
is their home number --

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:02.522 line:92%
right? -- their landline,

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:03.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
which people just generall
won't pick up.

00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:05.825 size:78% position:39%
I did find that once
the pandemic started,

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:08.561
on the first ring, people
were immediately picking u

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:11.197
and eager to tell you thei
secrets and their stories --

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:14.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
anything for some company.
-"The New York Times" reported

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:18.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you did write this book
in bed.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:21.007 size:78% position:39%
Is that a true fact?
-This is a true fact.

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:24.310
You'll notice I'm coming to yo
here from my nice home offic

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.081
that I share with my wife.
But my wife has a real job

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:33.419
And so when the pandemic hit
she started working from here.

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:36.222 line:92%
And I ended up working from bed.

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:39.926 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, this is about
the Sackler family

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:42.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
and their company,
Purdue Pharma,

00:02:42.295 --> 00:02:45.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
which was behind
the creation of OxyContin.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:49.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
And basically
the opioid epidemic

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:52.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
can be laid at their feet.
Now, one thing

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:54.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's fascinating
about reading this book is

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:58.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
from way, decades earlier,
when they first

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
got involved
in this pharmaceutical company

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:01.848 align:start size:94% position:6%
they always appreciated
the value

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:03.283 line:92%
of keeping the Sackler nam

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:07.687 size:84% position:42%
away from the actual
pharmaceutical company.

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:11.724
What was it that made them think
that was so valuable

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:13.526 line:92%
from, you know, years ago?

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This was the thing
I found so fascinating.

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:17.864 line:92%
Looking back, to some extent

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.733
the OxyContin story
from recent decades is familiar.

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.469
But, for me, looking back
at history of this family,

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:24.804 size:88% position:44% line:92%
going back to the 1950s,

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
you saw that there was
this weird thing

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:29.576 align:start size:94% position:6%
where they had a kind of mania
for putting their name

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:35.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
on buildings, on museums,
on university halls.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:37.283 align:start size:94% position:6%
My favorite example is,
if you go to London

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:40.820
to the Tate Modern, there'
an escalator you can ride on

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:41.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you get to the top
of the escalator

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:44.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
and there's a sign saying that
"You are riding

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.159
on the Sackler escalator."
So there is this tendency

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:49.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
to put the name on all of thes
philanthropic gifts

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:51.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
but to keep it off
of the family business.

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:53.633 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you have
this weird phenomenon

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
where I think, for a long time
there was a sense of this

00:03:55.602 --> 00:03:59.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
as this very generous, kind of
elite, wealthy dynasty.

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:02.709 align:start size:94% position:6%
But nobody really knew
where the money came from.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it turns out,
it's from this,

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:07.413 size:84% position:42%
I think, quite often,
sordid pharma business.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:11.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
-One of the things
that was fascinating to me

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:14.954 line:92%
because it's maybe even more -

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:16.956 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know if "insidious
is right word --

00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:19.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
but a more important invention
insofar as how

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:22.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
pharmaceutical companies
influence doctors,

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:26.332
is the Sackler family was really
at the forefront of that.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:31.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
They were an advertising famil
that basically started

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:33.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
advertising pharmaceutical
in a way

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we still see
very much today.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:39.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that something you knew
going into it?

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:41.881 size:78% position:39%
-It's not, and I was
so intrigued by this.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:43.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, Arthur Sackler
was the oldest

00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:46.753
of the original three brothers
He was, like, the Don Draper

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:48.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
of medical advertising
in the 1950s.

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:50.390 align:start size:94% position:6%
He had
a medical advertising firm

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:52.959
and he devised all these ways --
He basically realized

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:55.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the consumer,
like you or me,

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:57.664 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's not the person
you're trying to win over.

00:04:57.664 --> 00:04:59.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
What you want to win over
is the doctor

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:00.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
who's writing the prescription
and so he came up

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:03.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
with all these ways
of influencing doctors.

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:05.371 size:84% position:42%
And it's funny 'cause
if you talk to doctors,

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:06.472 size:75% position:38%
if you know doctors,
they'll often say,

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:09.008
"Oh, I would never be influenced
by a pharmaceutical company.

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:11.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
But to give an example
with Purdue Pharma,

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:13.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
this company spent
$9 million a year

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:17.617
just on buying food for doctors.
And you have to assume

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they were looking
at whether or not

00:05:19.052 --> 00:05:20.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
they were gonna get
a return on their investment

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:21.788 line:92%
And there are studies on this.

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:24.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
If they go out and buy
a steak dinner for a docto

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:28.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
or even a lunch on the go,
they can influence

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.630 line:92%
the way that doctor prescribes

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You even talk about this
in the book,

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:36.436
but, obviously, you had to
go into it expecting it.

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:38.938 align:start size:94% position:6%
It certainly came to pass
to be true.

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
You received
a great deal of blowback

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:43.042 size:88% position:44% line:92%
from the Sackler family.

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:46.479
Was it more than you even
expected, going into it?

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:48.781 size:75% position:38%
-More than I've ever
dealt with before.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:50.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, they really didn't wan
this book to be written.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they didn't
cooperate with me.

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:53.986 line:92%
Before I even started writing,

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.555 size:75% position:38%
they were sending me
these legal letters.

00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:56.923 size:78% position:39%
And it got to a point
where I was getting

00:05:56.923 --> 00:05:58.257
so many legal letters from them,
it reminded me

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:01.928
of that scene in "Harry Potter
where the letters are, like,

00:06:01.928 --> 00:06:04.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
coming in under the door
and down the chimney.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, I got a lot of them.
I'm still getting them.

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:09.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
We got one just last week
to the publisher.

00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:11.537 align:start size:94% position:6%
I keep threatening my wife
that I want to

00:06:11.537 --> 00:06:15.375
wallpaper our bathroom wit
legal letters from the Sacklers.

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:17.043 line:92%
We don't have quite enough yet

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:18.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, even though
the book is out,

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:22.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
they have not stopped trying
to what at this point?

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:26.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I think that part
of this story is

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:27.854 line:92%
about how they were this famil

00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:30.556 align:start size:94% position:6%
that made billions and billion
of dollars on OxyContin,

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:33.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
this drug that ends up
sparking the opioid crisis

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:35.094 align:start size:94% position:6%
But they were able
to kind of

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:36.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
write themselves
out of the picture.

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:38.631 size:75% position:38%
And I think that now
that that narrative

00:06:38.631 --> 00:06:40.166 size:84% position:42%
is getting corrected --
and it's not just me,

00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:41.701
it's other journalists, as well,
that are kind of

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:44.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
putting the family
back into that story,

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:46.038 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's something
that makes them pretty upset

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:47.440 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so they're doing
anything they can

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.844
to thwart people like me who try
and tell the truth of that

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:54.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
And when you're worth billions
and billions of dollars,

00:06:54.914 --> 00:06:56.015 line:92%
you have a lot of resources.

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:57.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
You can put a lot of peopl
on the job.

00:06:57.483 --> 00:07:00.019
-You also sort of mentione
that this book shouldn't b

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:03.623
the last word on it, but serve
as sort of a jumping-off point

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:06.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
for journalists to continu
to cover this story.

00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:09.695 align:start size:94% position:6%
Where would you like them
to explore next?

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:14.667
If you could sort of point
people who write about thi

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:16.068 size:78% position:39% line:92%
in any one direction?

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:18.704 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, the end game is actuall
playing out right now.

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
And weirdly enough,
it's happening

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:23.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
at a bankruptcy court
in New York.

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:24.477 line:92%
And before your eyes glaze over,

00:07:24.477 --> 00:07:27.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
the way in which this stor
is ending is

00:07:27.046 --> 00:07:29.682 size:84% position:42%
that the Sackler family
has taken $10 billion

00:07:29.682 --> 00:07:31.350 align:start size:94% position:6%
out of their company,
Purdue Pharma,

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.019
and when they've basically
taken all the money out,

00:07:33.019 --> 00:07:34.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
they've pushed the company
into bankruptcy,

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:36.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
and say, "Okay, well,
the company is bankrupt now,

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:38.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
but we're going to keep
our money."

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:40.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's in
that bankruptcy court

00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:41.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we're gonna see
how this ends,

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:44.530
whether they get a permanent
"get out of jail free" car

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
and there's a bar on any
future lawsuits against them

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:48.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's funny you mentioned
John Oliver.

00:07:48.768 --> 00:07:50.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
He had a line
that I always think about,

00:07:50.369 --> 00:07:52.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
years ago, in a broadcast,
where he said,

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:54.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
"If you want to do
something evil,

00:07:54.207 --> 00:07:55.708 line:92%
wrap it in something boring.

00:07:55.708 --> 00:07:58.544
And I feel like bankruptcy court
is the place

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:01.247 size:84% position:42%
where you can carry out
your villainous plans,

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:03.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it is
so confusing and complicated

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:06.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
But there's a new piece of
legislation in Congress

00:08:06.786 --> 00:08:08.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they've actually called
the Sackler Act,

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:10.323 align:start size:94% position:6%
which would prevent
the bankruptcy court

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:11.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
from letting the Sacklers
off the hook.

00:08:11.958 --> 00:08:13.893
And so that's pending right now,
and I think

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:15.962
journalists should be lookin
very closely at that case,

00:08:15.962 --> 00:08:17.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
because whether or not we ge
real accountability

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:20.099
for the Sacklers will all depend
on what happens

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:21.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
in that court
in the next few months.

00:08:21.901 --> 00:08:23.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, it's nice
that they're getting their nam

00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:24.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
on something else, you know?
-[ Chuckles ]

00:08:24.871 --> 00:08:28.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Not just escalators anymore
-Anymore.

00:08:28.975 --> 00:08:32.378 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, they're trading up.
-Hey, thank you.

00:08:32.378 --> 00:08:34.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
The book is fantastic.
No surprise there.

